cing him at 96-97 percent all point the same way. The only reasons for any caution are the single uncorroborated poll and the absence of expert ratings, so the margin is shaded slightly below the combined evidence rather than above it.", "watch": "A sharp shift in the national environment or a late surge of Republican outside spending could narrow the margin, though flipping the outcome would require an unusually large swing.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; fundamentals point clearly one way (post-redistricting), so it is treated as safe for that party."]}, "overview": "Utah's First District is an open seat shaped by redistricting, and its new boundaries lean twelve points Democratic. Democrat Ben McAdams and Republican Riley Owen debated repeatedly in late September, with coverage describing common ground on costs and artificial intelligence rather than a race changing character. The lone public poll, from early September, put McAdams ahead 63 to 21 among likely voters, though it has not been corroborated by other pollsters.\n\nMoney strongly favors McAdams, who has raised roughly $2.3 million and benefits from $2.7 million in outside support, while Owen has raised about $150,000. Betting markets give McAdams a 96 to 97 percent chance of winning. With the district's lean, the fundraising gap, polling, and markets all aligned, the main uncertainty here is the size of the margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9908, "margin": 20.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.99, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9909, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9899, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9886, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "202